<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">United States invasion of Panama</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1989">1989</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Operation Just Cause</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> invasion of Panama, code-named Operation Just Cause, began on 20 December 1989, when about 26,000 American troops attacked to overthrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-noriega">Manuel Noriega</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panamanian-defense-forces">Panama Defence Forces</a> were quickly destroyed, but hundreds of Panamanians were killed, many of them civilians, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-chorrillo">El Chorrillo</a> district burned. Noriega surrendered on 3 January 1990, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guillermo-endara">Guillermo Endara</a>, winner of the annulled 1989 election, became president.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-states-invasion-of-panama.jpg" alt="United States invasion of Panama" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LLs; derivative work by Georgfotoart · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Operation_Just_Cause_b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> kept numerous military bases and a substantial garrison in the Canal Zone to protect the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a>. On 7 September 1977 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-carter">Jimmy Carter</a> and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omar-torrijos">Omar Torrijos</a>, Panama's de facto leader, signed the Torrijos-Carter Treaties, which began the handover of the canal to Panama by 2000. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-noriega">Manuel Noriega</a>, Torrijos's successor, had been a paid informant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-intelligence-agency">Central Intelligence Agency</a> from 1967.</p><p>In the mid-1980s relations deteriorated after the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-spadafora">Hugo Spadafora</a> and the resignation of President Nicolas Ardito Barletta, and in 1988 federal grand juries indicted Noriega on drug-related charges. Several neighbouring leaders tried in secret to persuade him to resign and go into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, without success. After the general election of 7 May 1989, which the opposition candidate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guillermo-endara">Guillermo Endara</a> appeared to have won, Noriega annulled the result.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>S. Marine lieutenant, Robert Paz, was shot dead by the Panama Defense Forces. President George H. W. Bush ordered the execution of Operation Just Cause on 17 December. m. S. citizens, defending democracy and human rights, combating drug trafficking and protecting the integrity of the Torrijos-Carter Treaties. Forces of the Army, Navy, Marines, Air Force and Coast Guard took part. Panamanian forces were quickly overwhelmed, although operations went on for several weeks.</p><p>Endara was sworn in as president, in hiding, on the night before the invasion. Noriega took refuge in the diplomatic mission of the Holy See in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-city">Panama City</a> and surrendered on 3 January 1990. He was flown to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, tried, convicted and sentenced to 40 years in prison.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>S. S. civilians. About 20,000 people lost their homes in the urban fighting, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> paid $6,500 each to about 2,700 families displaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-chorrillo">El Chorrillo</a> fire. The Panama Defense Forces were dissolved. On 29 December 1989 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> voted 75 to 20, with 40 abstentions, to condemn the invasion as a flagrant violation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organization-of-american-states">Organization of American States</a> denounced it on 22 December.</p><p>A draft Security Council resolution demanding withdrawal was vetoed on 23 December by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the United Kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. S. government invoked self-defence, while several scholars have argued that the invasion was illegal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “United States invasion of Panama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In December 1989 the United States invaded in <b>Operation Just Cause</b>, destroyed the Defence Forces and captured Noriega.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>US forces launched <b>Operation Just Cause</b> to remove Panama's military ruler Manuel Noriega, who was wanted in the US on drug trafficking charges.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-20">20 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
